e bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**River Decision Analysis: King-High Showdown**\n\nOkay, here's the situation: I've got King of Spades, Deuce of Hearts. The board is Ace of Hearts, Eight of Diamonds, Eight of Hearts, Nine of Hearts, Six of Spades. We're on the river, and it's my turn. Blinds are probably 50/100, and I've got a small pot of 200 to contend with. I'm down a bit in the overall match, but that's irrelevant here. My King high is the best kicker on the board's pair of eights.\n\nThe opponent has been passively checking throughout. I've got to make a decision. I'm essentially playing the board – Eight-Eight-Ace-King-Nine. My opponent, based on their check-check line, probably doesn't have an Ace or an Eight, or they'd have shown aggression earlier. My King is the best kicker. Any Queen or Jack is beat. A ten or lower is beat. I lose to any pair higher than an eight.\n\nI could bet, but what am I getting called by? Lower pairs might call, and an Ace will definitely call. I don't need to bluff here. If I bet, I might push away a King high which would chop the pot. There's a minimal pot to try to get larger, and if I bet, I'm at risk of being raised and losing more chips. Given their passive play, checking looks like the best play. I get to see showdown for free and likely win a small pot. If they bet, I'll likely check-fold because their play signals they woke up with something decent.\n\n**Evaluation:** My hand has reasonable showdown value, but it's not strong enough to value bet the tiny pot. The opponent's passive play history suggests my hand is good enough to win.\n**Reason:** Betting risks re-opening action against better hands, and I don't get called by worse. Checking gets me to showdown for minimal risk.\n**Answer:** Check (0)\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: You hold the King of Spades and 2 of Hearts. The community cards are Ace of Hearts, 8 of Diamonds, 8 of Hearts, 9 of Hearts, and 6 of Spades. Your best five-card hand is a pair of 8s with Ace, King, and 9 kickers (playing the board with your King as a kicker). The pot is small (200 chips), and the opponent has shown passivity by checking on the Flop and Turn. Your hand beats Q-high and lower, chops with other K-high hands, and loses to any pair or Ace. Betting here has little utility: worse hands will likely fold, and better hands will call. Checking is the optimal strategy to realize your showdown value for free.\n\nReason: There is no value in betting as worse hands (like Q-high) will fold and better hands (like any pair or Ace) will call. Checking allows you to get to the showdown without risking more chips, giving you a chance to win the pot if the opponent holds a weaker high card.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
