st to call the raise.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\n1.  **Hand Strength:** K4o is weak. Raising or All-in would likely put me in a very bad position and waste chips.\n2.  **Pot Control:** Calling the Big Blind keeps the pot size small, allowing me to see the flop cheaply and evaluate my hand\'s potential.\n3.  **Position:** If I am the SB, I have position after the flop and if I check the BB, I will be out of position after the flop.\n4.  **Opponent Read:** At this early stage of the game, I have limited information about my opponent\'s tendencies. Playing conservatively allows for more observation and strategic adjustment in future hands.\n\n**Answer:** 0\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: K4o is a marginal hand in heads-up play, holding approximately 53% equity against a random hand. While it has decent high-card value, it plays poorly in large pots and is easily dominated by better Kings. You are likely in the Small Blind position (acting first pre-flop), meaning you have to call 50 chips to see a pot of 150 (3:1 odds), which requires only 25% equity.\n\nReason: Given the pot odds, folding is incorrect as you have sufficient equity. Raising with K4o turns your hand into a bluff that doesn't play well if called. The most prudent play is to Call (0) to see the flop cheaply, keeping the pot manageable while retaining the advantage of position post-flop.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
